speechtext:
I do not think that would be a difficult matter, Mr. Speaker. As a matter of fact, in a debate of this kind, although the rules do not provide that the mover of the resolution should close the debate, I believe he could do so with the consent of the house. I do not believe the house would at any time refuse to give that consent to whichever minister was moving the motion. But then again, that is merely a matter of procedure;
Emergency Powers Act and surely we should deal with procedural matters in such a way as to facilitate the business of the house.
I would much prefer to have heard the Prime Minister (Mr. St. Laurent) make a statement on this matter because it would help me to make up my mind whether there is reason for continuing this legislation at the present time. I cannot follow the Leader of the Opposition (Mr. Drew) in his violent objection to continuing it now because that objection was not put forward at the time the emergency powers were adopted in 1951, nor at the time of their extension in 1952. The world situation has not greatly changed since that time. But there is a point on which I agree with the Leader of the Opposition.

I wonder sometimes why this government pursues this course. Why does it ask for powers such as these?
(a)	control and suppression of maps, plans and photographs;
(b)	control of communications and means of communication;
Why ask for the power to control harbours, ports and territorial waters? We can understand one or two of these. If we were in a situation of grave danger we could understand these powers being asked for, but we now know that our situation is just post-Korea. Then they ask for the power to control transportation by land, air or water and the control of the transport of persons and things. What more absolute powers have ever been asked for in the days of war than these? Then they ask for the power to control trade, exportation, importation, production and manufacture.
